Hi,

I'm experiencing problems with my Promise SATAII TX4 controller, quite
similar to what Kim reported a few months ago.

Using a fresh linux-2.6.12.5 upstream kernel to boot into a rescue shell
(so there's no distribution specific stuff going on, nothing else
loaded, etc.), I can access my three connected Maxtor 120GB drives just
fine, as long as I do so individually.

   e.g.
   $ dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/null
   $ dd if=/dev/sdb of=/dev/null
   $ dd if=/dev/sdc of=/dev/null

Accessing 2 drives concurrently

   e.g.
   $ dd if=/dev/sda of=/dev/null &
   $ dd if=/dev/sdb of=/dev/null &

is fine too, but as soon as I concurrently start accessing a third drive
as well (in whatever order, it's not specific to sdc), the system starts
to get in serious trouble:

Either immediately or after just a few seconds, I can hear some kind of
"clonk" sound (I've physically disconnected all other drives, so it's
probably one/some/all of the drives) and just after that, the kernel
dumps something like this:

irq 4: nobody cared!
[<c01033e6>] dump_stack+0x17/0x19
[<c012df23>] __report_bad_irq+0x27/0x83
[<c012dfff>] note_interrupt+0x5d/0x7e
[<c012dbbf>] __do_IRQ+0xbf/0xd0
[<c01046f0>] do_IRQ+0x1c/0x28
[<c0102f22>] common_interrupt+0x1a/0x20
[<c0117729>] do_softirq+0x2a/0x2c
[<c01046f5>] do_IRQ+0x21/0x28
[<c0102f22>] common_interrupt+0x1a/0x20
handlers:
[<c024a068>] (pdc_interrupt+0x0/0x15c)
Disabling IRQ #4

and, naturally, everything starts falling apart shortly afterwards:

ata2: command timeout
ATA: abnormal status 0xFF on port 0xE080029C
ata2: status=0xff { Busy }
SCSI error: <1 0 0 0> return code = 0x8000002
sdb: Current: sense key=0xb
    ASC=0x47 ASCQ=0x0
end_request: I/O error, dev sdb, sector 1297600
Buffer I/O error on device sdb, logical block 162200
ata4: command timeout
ata4: status=0x51 { DriveReady SeekComplete Error }
ata4: called with no error (51)!
SCSI error : <3 0 0 0> return code = 0x8000002
sdc: Current: sense key=0x3
[...]

I've attached my configuration file, the lspci -vv output and
/proc/interrupts as well as /proc/scsi/scsi output (the latter three
were produced running my regular debian 2.6.12-1-686 kernel though).


I'm not sure what causes the problems, as I definitely had success
synchronising my RAID5 array at the very beginning of the installation
of this card (~01.08.2005) and the first breakage surfaced almost two
weeks later during the nightly backup (lots of read traffic -> interrupt
disabled -> RAID broken/disabled -> system hang).

So, what I'm probably trying to say is, I don't know whether this
couldn't be some kind of hardware problem instead. Originally I was
using a SiI based controller, which apparently caused the connected
drives to rapidly develop bad sectors (in 4 cases they remained broken
during an analysis from another system and in 1 case it was somehow
specific to the SiI controllers "view" of the drive *shrug*).

Or maybe I was just very lucky and the bug was there all the time.


Now, if you have any idea what I could do/test/install/send you to clear
this up or fix the bug, please tell me! I'd really appreciate any kind
of help on this.

Thanks, Fabian


Some time ago, Kim Roland Rasmussen wrote:
> Hi all,
> 
> Together with a couple of others I have been bitten by problems with the
> Promise SATAII TX4 controller.
> 
> Common to all is that at some point the kernel log contains the "irq
> <SOME NUMBER>: nobody cared!" error message, followed by a call/stack
> trace. This is quickly followed up by a "Disabling IRQ #<SOME NUMBER>"
> which effectively kills the SATA controller and access to the disks.
> 
> Although a somewhat unusual request, could I ask somebody to look at
> this
> 
>     http://forums.gentoo.org/viewtopic-p-2247734.html
> 
> developing thread in the Gentoo Forums. There's simply to much
> information there to repost here.
> 
> In my case (posted under <rasmussen>) I temporarily seem to have gotten
> around it by replacing a driver with another of a different brand.
> However, this is not the case for others.
> 
> Any idea of what's going on? To me it looks like some interrupt isn't
> cleared or something like that (but I'm just guessing).
> 
> Cheers,
>   Kim

